[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering JSON-RPC, WebSockets, and Web3 Node Infrastructure"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interacting with blockchains like Ethereum requires specialized communication protocols and robust node infrastructure. This tutorial covers the foundational mechanics of JSON-RPC, real-time event streaming via WebSockets, and how to scale applications using Managed Node Providers like Infura and Alchemy."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding JSON-RPC in Web3"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSON-RPC is a stateless, lightweight remote procedure call (RPC) protocol encoded in JSON. In the context of EVM-compatible blockchains, clients communicate with nodes by sending JSON-RPC requests over HTTP or WebSockets to execute methods defined by the Ethereum JSON-RPC specification."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"curl https://mainnet.infura.io/v3/YOUR_INFURA_KEY \
-X POST \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"eth_blockNumber","params":[],"id":1}'"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Real-Time Data Streaming with WebSockets"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"While HTTP is sufficient for querying historical data or sending transactions, WebSockets are required for real-time applications. WebSockets maintain a persistent, bidirectional TCP connection, enabling nodes to push events (such as new blocks or smart contract logs) directly to your application without polling."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"const { ethers } = require('ethers');

const provider = new ethers.WebSocketProvider('wss://eth-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_ALCHEMY_KEY');

provider.on('block', (blockNumber) => {
  console.log('New Block Minted: ' + blockNumber);
});"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Managed Node Providers: Infura vs. Alchemy"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Running your own blockchain node requires significant disk space, network bandwidth, and maintenance overhead. Managed node infrastructure providers abstract this complexity away, offering highly scalable, reliable APIs."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Infura: One of the oldest and most dependable RPC providers, offering broad multi-chain support and seamless integration tools."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Alchemy: Known for advanced developer tooling like Alchemy Notify, Enhanced APIs for NFT tracking, and superior request tracing capabilities."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Best Practices for Production Architecture"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement Fallbacks: Never rely on a single node provider. Use libraries like ethers.js FallbackProvider to switch between Infura, Alchemy, and self-hosted nodes automatically."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Secure API Keys: Never expose your provider secret keys in frontend client code. Route requests through a backend proxy or configure domain whitelisting."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimize Rate Limits: Batch JSON-RPC requests using arrays when querying multiple independent data points to reduce connection overhead."}]
